module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Smooth
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Immersion
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Regular.RegularSequence

import Sheaf.RingTheory.SmoothQuotientRegularSequence
import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.RingHomProperties

/-!
# Sections of smooth morphisms are regular immersions

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 067R, lemma-section-smooth-regular-immersion
(blueprint: lem-sfb-smooth-section-regular-immersion).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A section of an arbitrary smooth morphism is an immersion and, near each point of
its source, is the quotient closed immersion of an affine scheme by a finite regular list.
The list may be empty and its length is allowed to vary with the point.

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 067R, lemma-section-smooth-regular-immersion
(blueprint: lem-sfb-smooth-section-regular-immersion).

Proof:
1. Since the composite of the section with the structure map is the identity, immersion
cancellation (lem-zl-immersion-cancellation) makes the section an immersion. Factor it as
 a closed immersion into an open subscheme of X (def-weil-closed-immersion).
2. Around the given point choose an affine open T in S and an affine open V in that open
subscheme of X intersected with the inverse image of T. The inverse image Z of V is affine
and is closed in V. Thus its coordinate map A → C is surjective; the section equation
makes it an R-algebra map, where T = Spec R (def-weil-affine-spectrum).
3. Both V → T and Z → T are smooth: the former by restriction of f, the latter because
Z is open in T (lem-pc-open-immersion-smooth-composition). The affine smoothness comparison
(lem-weil-pbc-smooth-ring-map-iff-smooth-affine) makes both R-algebras smooth.
4. At the prime of the image point apply lem-pur-smooth-quotient-regular-sequence to
A → C. After inverting an element outside that prime, its kernel is generated by a finite
regular list. The cited theorem allows arbitrary base rings and the empty list.
5. Take U to be this principal affine open. Localizing the surjection gives its quotient
presentation: a fraction maps to zero precisely when some power of the denominator times
its numerator lies in the original kernel. This identifies the inverse image of U with
the spectrum of the quotient by the regular list, compatibly with the actual restricted
section. Regularity includes the injectivity on successive quotients and the nonzero final
quotient (def-weil-pbc-regular-sequence), also witnessed by the given source point. -/
theorem smoothSection_regularImmersion
    {X S : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ S) [Smooth f] (s : S ⟶ X)
    (hs : s ≫ f = 𝟙 S) :
    IsImmersion s ∧ ∀ t : S, ∃ U : X.Opens, s t ∈ U ∧
      IsClosedImmersion (s ∣_ U) ∧
      ∃ (A : CommRingCat.{u}) (a : List A) (eU : U.toScheme ≅ Spec A)
        (eZ : (s ⁻¹ᵁ U).toScheme ≅ Spec (.of (A ⧸ Ideal.ofList a))),
        RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A a ∧
          eZ.hom ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Ideal.ofList a))) =
            (s ∣_ U) ≫ eU.hom := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
